; Format 3: the index of aligned phrases. For one language, the words and the phrases of its bridge texts that are aligned
; to original words, with the Strong keys they translate and, per Strong key and book, the distinct verses and the
; occurrences. For lookups across books. An optional unit (catalog.md): no unit needs it, it needs nothing, and building
; with or without it gives byte-equal segments of every other class.
;
; One segment per part. The parts split the keys by their start (normative): a part's "group" is a range of keys in code point
; order, written P-Q (or P for P-P): P and Q are prefixes of 1 to 6 characters from the lowercase ASCII letters and the space,
; the first a letter ("a", "ar-b", "the -thf"); the end Q of a range (and a lone prefix, which is P-P) also ends in a letter, never a space, or the group is "other". A key (after NFC and lowercase, below) is in the range P-Q
; iff P <= key and key.slice(0, len(Q)) <= Q, both by code point (a key shorter than Q is compared as it is). So the key "as" is
; in "ar-b" and the key "t" is in "t-td" and not in "ta-tz". The builder's table of parts is contiguous in code point order, with
; no overlap and no hole (the units of one catalog are a subset of it, in order and without overlap, since a part with no key is not built; that is a rule of the builder, and a reader does not check it), so a key whose characters after the first are not letters ("a'x") falls into the range of its
; neighbours in the table. The partition table is the builder's and is not in the segment: a reader gets the ranges from the catalog, from
; the ids of its "aligned:{part}" units. The part of a key is the aligned unit of the catalog whose range holds it; if no
; unit's range holds it, "aligned:other". "other" thus holds every key outside all the listed ranges (a first character that
; is not a-z: "¿", "Ἰ", a digit; or beyond the last range). A part whose range holds no key is not built.
;
; Checks of "keys" against "group" (tests/README.md): for a range group, a segment alone is enough (every key lies in the
; range). For "other" the check needs the catalog (no key may lie in a range of the catalog's aligned units); a reader that
; has the segment alone does not make it and does not fail.

aligned-segment = body<aligned-header, aligned-tables>

aligned-header = {
  "class": "aligned",
  "v": 1,
  "tables": { * table-name => [uint, bool] },
  "lang": tstr,
  "group": aligned-group,      ; the part
  "res": [+ [tstr, tstr]],     ; the bridge texts the entries came from (resource key and release tag), e.g. the
                               ; language's GLT and GST; the same in every part of one build
}

; A prefix (no trailing space), a range of two prefixes (P not after Q by code point; Q has no trailing space) or "other". Another text is E_BODY, like a malformed group
; of "lex" (the range's order is not in the regexp: "d-b" is E_BODY too).
aligned-group = tstr .regexp "[a-z][a-z ]{0,5}-[a-z]([a-z ]{0,4}[a-z])?|[a-z]([a-z ]{0,4}[a-z])?|other"

; How the entries are made (normative; the same rules as the measurement script tests/measure/alignedIndex.ts).
; - Texts: the bridge texts of the book segments whose resource ends in /glt, /gst (es-419) or /ult, /ust (en). One
;   index per language, all its texts together.
; - A word entry: each word piece whose "align" list is not empty. A phrase entry: each maximal run of two or more
;   consecutive word pieces (only " " pieces between them) whose "align" lists are equal and not empty, the texts joined
;   with single spaces.
; - The key (the one exception to "text is kept as published", container.md): the text in NFC, then lowercased with toLocaleLowerCase(lang). A reader never normalizes (container.md):
;   a caller who looks a key up normalizes and lowercases it the same way before it compares bytes.
; - The Strong keys of an entry: the strongKey of the original words at the aligned positions (ids.md); a word with no
;   Strong key is skipped; an entry with none is not stored.
; - Per Strong key and book: the distinct verses (a verse event with a range "1-2" counts as the verse of its first
;   number) and the occurrences. An occurrence is one appearance of the entry in a text, counted once for each distinct
;   Strong key of its aligned positions. Each text adds its own occurrences; a verse in two texts is one verse. So a
;   phrase found in one text only has its verse once and the occurrences of that text. A phrase may run across a verse
;   event; it counts in the verse of its first word.
aligned-tables = {
  "keys": texts,               ; required, v1; the keys, sorted by code point, no repeat
  "strongs": strtab,           ; required, v1; the Strong keys the entries use, each once (a text twice is E_BODY); the builder writes the most used first
  "data": uvarints,            ; required, v1; per key, in the order of "keys", these numbers one after the other:
                               ;   s = number of Strong keys of the entry, then s times, with the Strong keys in code
                               ;   point order of their text:
                               ;     the index of the Strong key in "strongs",
                               ;     b = number of books, then b times, with the books ascending:
                               ;       book delta (from the previous book of this Strong key, the first from 0; so the
                               ;         first is the book number and the book number never passes 66),
                               ;       verses (distinct verses of that book),
                               ;       occurrences (never less than verses)
}

; Builder rules a reader does not check: the Strong keys of an entry are in code point order of their text; the "strongs"
; table has none unused and the most used first. A builder never writes an entry with no Strong key (s = 0) or a Strong key
; with no book (b = 0), and a reader rejects both (E_BODY), as it does a text twice in "strongs", a key empty or repeated, and
; a Strong index repeated in one entry (tests/README.md).
